 "details": {
  "title": "Arban – Ornament Study No. 59",
  "lead": "From Arban's Grande méthode complète de cornet à pistons et de saxhorn, first edition, 1864. The notes are the cornet part as printed, in written pitch for the B♭ cornet. Below: the book's own words, translated from the French.",
  "facts": [
   [
    "Book",
    "Arban, Grande méthode complète de cornet à pistons et de saxhorn, first edition (Paris: Léon Escudier, 1864)"
   ],
   [
    "Section",
    "Ornaments"
   ],
   [
    "Page in the book",
    "111"
   ],
   [
    "Tempo marking",
    "Andante moderato"
   ],
   [
    "Layout",
    "Each single small note is the note before it, sounded again and carried to the next note by sliding the sound. The four small notes after each long note are played quickly at its end. The figures 1 2 3 under the small B flat in bar 11 are the valves the book asks for there."
   ]
  ],
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "The portamento",
    "paragraphs": [
     "The portamento is a small note that is, in fact, only the repetition of some note that you want to carry to another by sliding the sound. Do not overuse this kind of ornament, for it would become bad taste; used sparingly it can be very effective. But I much prefer the carried sound, without the help of the small note. (See Nos. 55 to 59.)"
    ]
   },
   {
    "heading": "A word on all of these",
    "paragraphs": [
     "Since all the lessons on ornaments were written to serve as studies, I have brought together, on purpose and in abundance, every kind of ornament. But take great care not to overuse them so in practice, for that would be in the worst of taste."
    ]
   }
  ],
  "sources": [
   {
    "title": "IMSLP: Grande méthode complète de cornet à pistons (Arban), first edition, Paris 1864",
